<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The two strings Android shows on the Accessibility settings screen, where the
  user decides whether to grant the most sensitive permission on the platform.
  Without them the screen offers no label and no explanation at all.

  The label IS the name the user hunts for in a list of a dozen services. A
  generic default reads as someone else's app: a user in Spain spent nineteen
  days looking for TiedSiren in that list, on six app versions, while the line
  sat there under another name. He never once completed the grant. Whatever
  ships here must be the name the user already knows.

  The description is the whole case for the grant. It names what the service
  reads, because Play requires the disclosure — but in one clause, because this
  is read standing in a settings screen, not studied.

  A host app can still override both: declaring a string with the same name wins
  the resource merge, without touching this library. See CONFIGURATION.md.
-->
<resources>
  <string name="expo_accessibility_service_label">TiedSiren</string>
  <string name="expo_accessibility_service_description">Lets TiedSiren see which app or website you open, and block the ones you chose.</string>
</resources>
